  "memoryHacks": [
    {
      "title": "1. Start with the Present",
      "body": "Master Présent first — French uses one present form for both 'I speak' and 'I am speaking'. Regular -ER verbs all share one pattern (-e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent)."
    },
    {
      "title": "2. Embrace the Big Irregulars",
      "body": "Tackle être, avoir, aller, faire, pouvoir, vouloir, and prendre early. They appear in almost every French conversation and unlock compound tenses."
    },
    {
      "title": "3. Passé Composé: Avoir vs Être",
      "body": "Most verbs build the past with avoir (j'ai parlé, j'ai fait). Movement verbs like aller and venir use être (je suis allé, je suis venu) and agree with gender/number."
    },
    {
      "title": "4. Passé Composé vs Imparfait",
      "body": "Use passé composé for completed events (J'ai fait un gâteau) and imparfait for habits or ongoing background scenes (Ils allaient au parc tous les jours)."
    }
  ],
  "verbs": [
